Task 1.3 – Design Defence (1 Mark)
The redesigned program is easier to test and maintain because the GUI and payment logic are separated. The FeeWindow class handles only user interactions, while FeeService handles validation and database operations. Each class can be tested independently. Changes to the GUI do not affect database logic. Background processing prevents the window from freezing. Separate event handlers also make the code easier to understand and modify.
Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
Instead of using one listener with an if-else chain, each button can have its own listener object or Swing Action.
1. Using separate listeners
pay.addActionListener(e -> makePayment());

clear.addActionListener(e -> {
    regNo.setText("");
    amount.setText("");
});

receipt.addActionListener(e -> showReceipt());


Each button directly invokes its own handler. There is no need to compare the action command against every button name.
2. Complexity comparison
Feature	One listener with if-else	Separate listeners
Event dispatch	O(k) worst case	O(1) per button
Comparisons	Up to k comparisons	No action-command search
Space	O(1) listener object	O(k) listener objects
Maintenance	More complex as buttons increase	Easier to maintain
Code structure	One common handler	Separate handlers

Here, k represents the number of buttons.
3. Analysis
- One listener: The program checks action commands one by one. In the worst case, it performs k comparisons. Therefore, time complexity is O(k).
- Separate listeners: Each button directly calls its assigned handler. Event dispatch is O(1) with respect to the number of buttons.
- Space complexity: If every button has its own listener object, the total listener storage is O(k). Each listener requires O(1) space.
- Swing Action: A separate Action can also be assigned to each button. It helps reuse the same action in multiple interface components.
Conclusion: Separate listeners or Swing Actions are better for applications with many buttons because they improve readability, organization and maintenance.

Final Result
The poor fee-payment design was analysed and redesigned using Java Swing. The improved design separates GUI operations from payment logic, validates user input, uses PreparedStatement to prevent SQL injection, closes database resources automatically, and performs database work using SwingWorker so the interface remains responsive. Separate listeners improve event handling and make the application easier to maintain.
